Machine Learning and Retrieval-Augmented Generation
Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence in which computers learn patterns from data and use
those patterns to make predictions or decisions. The main types of machine learning are supervised learning,
unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning.
Supervised learning uses labeled training data. Each training example contains an input and a known target
output. Common supervised learning tasks include classification and regression. Classification predicts a
category, while regression predicts a continuous numerical value.
Unsupervised learning works with data that does not have labeled target values. It is often used to discover
hidden patterns or groups in data. Clustering is a common unsupervised learning technique in which similar
data points are grouped together.
A Support Vector Machine, or SVM, is a supervised machine learning algorithm. It can be used for
classification and regression. For classification, an SVM attempts to find a decision boundary that separates
different classes. The algorithm focuses on important training examples called support vectors. One
advantage of SVMs is that they can work well in high-dimensional spaces. A disadvantage is that training can
become expensive for very large datasets.
Natural Language Processing, or NLP, is a field of artificial intelligence that focuses on processing and
understanding human language. NLP applications include text classification, machine translation, question
answering, summarization, and chatbots.
Retrieval-Augmented Generation, commonly called RAG, combines information retrieval with text generation.
Instead of relying only on information stored in a language model, a RAG system first retrieves relevant
information from an external knowledge source. The retrieved information is then provided to the language
model as context for generating an answer.
A typical RAG pipeline has several stages. First, documents are loaded and divided into smaller pieces called
chunks. Each chunk is converted into a numerical representation called an embedding. Embeddings allow
semantically similar pieces of text to be compared using vector similarity.
A vector database stores embeddings and allows a system to search for chunks that are relevant to a user's
question. FAISS is a library designed for efficient similarity search over dense vectors. During retrieval, the
user's question is also converted into an embedding. The system compares the query embedding with stored
embeddings and returns the most relevant chunks.
Keyword-based retrieval can also be used in a RAG system. BM25 is a traditional information retrieval
method that ranks documents based on the occurrence and importance of query terms. BM25 can be useful
when exact keywords or technical terms are important.
Hybrid retrieval combines multiple retrieval strategies. For example, a system can combine BM25 keyword
retrieval with vector similarity retrieval. Reciprocal Rank Fusion, or RRF, can then combine the rankings from
the different retrieval methods into one final ranking.
A conversational RAG system keeps track of previous messages in a conversation. This allows a user to ask
follow-up questions such as "What are its advantages?" without repeating the topic. The system can rewrite a
follow-up question into a standalone question before retrieving information. This improves retrieval because
the rewritten question contains the necessary context.
Contextual compression is a retrieval technique that reduces unnecessary information before the final answer
is generated. It attempts to keep the parts of retrieved documents that are most relevant to the user's
question.
Source citation is another important part of a RAG application. After retrieving documents, the system can
return information about the source file and page associated with the retrieved chunks. This helps users
understand where an answer came from and makes the system easier to verify.
A good RAG system should answer questions using retrieved context and avoid inventing information that is
not supported by the available documents. If the required information is not present in the knowledge base,
the system should clearly state that it does not know the answer